    <briefdescription>
<para><computeroutput><ref refid="class_alloc_logger" kindref="compound">AllocLogger</ref></computeroutput> / <computeroutput><ref refid="class_stats_allocator" kindref="compound">StatsAllocator</ref></computeroutput> <mdash/> single-threaded debug/benchmark instrumentation. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Declares <computeroutput><ref refid="class_alloc_logger" kindref="compound">AllocLogger</ref></computeroutput> with static counters (<computeroutput>mNumAllocs</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mAllocSize</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mNumDeallocs</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mCurrentAllocSize</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mHighWater</computeroutput>) and the <computeroutput><ref refid="class_stats_allocator" kindref="compound">StatsAllocator</ref></computeroutput> wrapper that bumps those counters on every allocation / deallocation. The class is intentionally minimal: counters are static so per-type statistics share a collector, and there is no locking on the counter increments (the in-source comment is explicit: &quot;not
thread safe&quot;). <computeroutput><ref refid="class_stats_allocator_a230d777fb803ccbd6ffe5204618c3310_1a230d777fb803ccbd6ffe5204618c3310" kindref="member">StatsAllocator::allocate</ref></computeroutput> / <computeroutput>deallocate</computeroutput> log the size, then call straight through to <computeroutput>::operator new</computeroutput> / <computeroutput>::operator delete</computeroutput>; there is no free-skipping debug mode. It is for debugging and benchmarking only <mdash/> not safe in any path the supervisor parallelises.</para>
<para>Production allocations route through <computeroutput><ref refid="newdelete_8hpp" kindref="compound">newdelete.hpp</ref></computeroutput>&apos;s <computeroutput><ref refid="structour__new__delete" kindref="compound">our_new_delete</ref></computeroutput> (Boehm GC). The legacy <computeroutput><ref refid="mem_8hpp" kindref="compound">mem.hpp</ref></computeroutput> size-class <computeroutput><ref refid="classstash" kindref="compound">stash</ref></computeroutput> allocator is now stubbed (its <computeroutput>new_elem</computeroutput> / <computeroutput>delete_elem</computeroutput> short-circuit to <computeroutput><ref refid="newdelete_8hpp_af464ed09a8bd05599f0a0f8ae9124228_1af464ed09a8bd05599f0a0f8ae9124228" kindref="member">newarray_clear</ref></computeroutput> / <computeroutput><ref refid="m2-mem_8cpp_a1c6306973edadb50294a656e3927fcba_1a1c6306973edadb50294a656e3927fcba" kindref="member">freemem</ref></computeroutput>), so <computeroutput><ref refid="class_alloc_logger" kindref="compound">AllocLogger</ref></computeroutput> is the only opt-in counter source left <mdash/> reach for it when you want to count allocations of a single type in a benchmark run and dump the totals afterward.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="see"><para><ref refid="newdelete_8hpp" kindref="compound">newdelete.hpp</ref> </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="see"><para><ref refid="mem_8hpp" kindref="compound">mem.hpp</ref> </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
